(*
L3 — timers as channel events. No new semantics: a timer is an event source that posts
into the space, exactly like the Milestone-D host seam. Covers most "wait then act"
without any suspension; with fsm.ns it covers protocol timeouts.

REQUIRED C SEAM (work order — mirrors the Milestone-D event-source seam):
  platform timerAfter: ms token: tok      -> one-shot; on expiry the seam posts the
                                             pending (channel, message) registered under tok
  platform timerEvery: ms token: tok      -> repeating
  platform timerCancel: tok               -> best-effort cancel; already-fired is a no-op
  platform monotonicMillis                -> ms since boot (64-bit int; see integers.md)

Design constraints honored:
  - The C side carries DATA (token, ms), not policy: which channel, what message, and
    cancellation bookkeeping live here in nsl. C fires "token N expired" into a single
    reserved internal channel; this module owns the fan-out. So the seam stays one
    dumb registry + one posting path.
  - Timer delivery is an ordinary reactor dispatch: run-to-completion ordering (T-C.6)
    applies to timer events identically. No ISR-context nsl execution, ever.
  - A Timer token is a small capability: holding it = the right to cancel.

Wiring at boot (supervisor setup):
  timers:: Timers usingSpace: space seam: platform.
  space whenever: 'sys/timer' do: [:e | timers dispatch: e].   (* reserved channel —
    landed spelling is plain 'sys/timer', no '%' sigil; see kernel.ns and the
    Milestone D sync note in ../tests/README.md *)

Usage:
  t:: timers after: 50 post: (Record tag: #timeout fields: (Map fromPairs: (OrderedCollection new))) on: sensorChan.
  timers cancel: t.
  timers every: 1000 post: #tick on: tickChan.

SYNC NOTE (Milestone B landed 2026-07-06): the Usage line above used to say
`Map empty`, but the landed kernel.ns Map has no zero-arg/`empty` class-side
constructor (`with:` sugar needs >=1 pair). Spelling "no fields" instead as
`fromPairs:` over an empty collection is the verified equivalent — see
`nsl-tests/08_messages/05_map.ns`'s own note on `fromPairs:`'s input being a
plain (unvalidated) `size`/`at:` container, e.g. OrderedCollection.
*)
class TimersStaging usingPlatform: platform = () (

  class Timers = (
    | space seam nextToken ::= 1. pending ::= Dictionary new. |
    (* pending: token -> Pending(channel, message, repeating) *)
  ) (
    class Pending = ( | chan msg repeating | ) (
      public chan = ( ^ chan )  public msg = ( ^ msg )  public repeating = ( ^ repeating )
      setChan: c msg: m repeating: r = ( chan:: c. msg:: m. repeating:: r. ^ self )
    ) : ( public chan: c msg: m repeating: r = ( ^ self new setChan: c msg: m repeating: r ) )

    public after: ms post: aMessage on: aChannel = (
      | tok |
      tok:: self allocateToken.
      pending at: tok put: (Pending chan: aChannel msg: aMessage repeating: false).
      seam timerAfter: ms token: tok.
      ^ tok
    )

    public every: ms post: aMessage on: aChannel = (
      | tok |
      tok:: self allocateToken.
      pending at: tok put: (Pending chan: aChannel msg: aMessage repeating: true).
      seam timerEvery: ms token: tok.
      ^ tok
    )

    public cancel: tok = (
      | p |
      p:: pending at: tok ifAbsent: [ nil ].       (* cancelling twice is a no-op *)
      p == nil ifFalse: [
        pending removeKey: tok.
        seam timerCancel: tok ].
      ^ self
    )

    public now = ( ^ seam monotonicMillis )

    (* Called by the supervisor's reserved-channel reaction. Event = the token (int).
       Late fire after cancel -> not in `pending` -> dropped silently BY DESIGN
       (transient-signal semantics; the race is inherent, the resolution is pinned). *)
    public dispatch: tokenEvent = (
      | p |
      p:: pending at: tokenEvent ifAbsent: [ nil ].
      p == nil ifFalse: [
        p repeating ifFalse: [ pending removeKey: tokenEvent ].
        p chan out: '' value: p msg ].    (* '' = post at the capability's own root *)
      ^ self
    )

    allocateToken = ( | t | t:: nextToken. nextToken:: nextToken + 1. ^ t )
    setSpace: s seam: c = ( space:: s. seam:: c. ^ self )
  ) : (
    public usingSpace: s seam: c = ( ^ self new setSpace: s seam: c )
  )
) : ( )

(* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   WHO OWNS THE BASE TICK — rete#231 D1, added 2026-08-22 after the board run.

   Everything above describes timers an APP registers. One timer is not the app's:
   the kernel registers a repeating base tick of its own, and that changes what the
   absence of a heartbeat is allowed to mean.

   THE ARGUMENT, and it is not about cost. A heartbeat that a wedged node can still
   emit is `port=up` with better manners — the exact lie rete#152 is about. So the
   heartbeat must ride THIS module's reactor dispatch rather than a C thread of its
   own: if the VM is stuck in the interpreter, the tick stops, and the stopping is
   the signal. Put the heartbeat on a separate C thread and it keeps beating over a
   dead reactor, which is worse than no heartbeat because it is believed.

   It also adds NO C. `timer_target_snapshot()` already reports every live repeating
   timer to the rete#215 observer, so one kernel-owned `timers every:` feeds both
   consumers at once: it drives the check-in AND it puts a deadline in the snapshot.

   WHY THAT SECOND HALF MATTERS. `observer_verdict()` opens with

       int n = timer_target_snapshot(period, since, OBS_MAX_TIMERS);
       if (n == 0) return COND_NONE;   /* nothing to judge it against */

   so before this rung a node whose app declared no repeating timer was UNJUDGEABLE.
   Not "judged healthy" — never judged at all. That is the ordinary shape for a
   purely reactive sensor (GPIO, inbound radio), and it is invisible in the worst
   way, because `pulse_pump` is K_FOREVER-blocking on Zephyr and therefore looks
   identical whether the node is healthily idle or wedged solid. With the kernel's
   tick always present, `n` is never 0 and the deadline always exists.

   THE INSTANCE RULE IS LOAD-BEARING, and it is the one way to get this wrong.
   `platform timers` answers a FRESH Timers instance on every send, while the C
   registry underneath is keyed globally. A kernel that minted its own instance
   would register a token the app's `dispatch:` never heard of, and every tick would
   be dropped in silence. So the bridge is HANDED the app's one instance:

       bridge startTicking: timers on: tickCh

   Corollary worth stating, because it bit on the first board run: the tick is
   registered BY the bridge, and the bridge needs a socket. A node that fails its
   UDP bind constructs no bridge, registers no tick, and lands back in the n == 0
   hole — silent, unwedgeable and unjudgeable. Whatever registers the base tick must
   not be downstream of anything that can fail at boot.

   MEASURED ON HARDWARE, 2026-08-22 (`liveness-heartbeat`, c6spare, esp32c6):
   TimerlessNodeC6 declares no timer at all; the kernel's 2000 ms tick was the only
   one in the image. The node wedged deliberately at tick 15 and the observer fired
   with `deadline=16000ms` — `shortest_period × WEDGE_SLACK` = 2000 × 8, a number
   that exists ONLY because the kernel registered that tick. Between the wedge and
   the reset there were 18.03 s and ZERO console lines of any kind: the heartbeat did
   not outlive the wedge, which is the whole claim. Boot 2 reported `pass=2 fail=0`
   and ran on clean through the 180 s observation window.

   CAUTION — TWO EIGHTS THAT ARE NOT THE SAME EIGHT. The observer's deadline is
   `shortest_period × WEDGE_SLACK` (slack 8, in observer.c). The budget the node
   PUBLISHES to porta at `obs/liveness` is `cadence × 8`, chosen by the app. Both
   came to 16000 ms here and neither is derived from the other. Change one and the
   other does not follow.

   NOT GATED BY ANY OF THE ABOVE: D3's coalescing claim — that two obligations at
   different multiples of one base tick cost fewer radio wakes than the sum of
   independent periods — needs a second obligation and a wake counter, and the node
   that proved the rest has one obligation and no counter.
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *)
